<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Cleartext policy, moved out of the manifest so it is reviewable rather than a
  single boolean.

  This replaces android:usesCleartextTraffic="true". A network security config
  supersedes that attribute, and unlike the attribute it can say different things
  about different hosts, which is the point.

  ## Why cleartext is permitted at all

  Nightbell monitors endpoints the user types in. A large share of what people
  point it at has no certificate and never will: a NAS admin page on a LAN
  address, a service on a homelab box reached over Tailscale, a container on
  http://192.168.1.20:8080, a printer. Refusing cleartext would not make those
  endpoints safer, it would make them unmonitorable, and the user would go back to
  not knowing when they break.

  ## Why it cannot be scoped to the user's domains

  A reviewer asked for cleartext to be restricted to user-configured domains,
  which is the right instinct and is not expressible here. This file is compiled
  into the APK at build time. The domains are typed months later on someone's
  phone, and there is no runtime API to add a domain-config entry. Android offers
  no per-request cleartext opt-in either: NetworkSecurityPolicy is consulted by
  OkHttp per host, and if the policy says no for a host then that host cannot be
  checked at all.

  So base-config permits it, and the compensating controls are elsewhere: the app
  asks for no location, camera, contacts or storage permission, it has no
  analytics and no crash reporter, it sends the user's credentials only to the host
  the user named, and the setup screen shows the scheme of the URL being saved.

  ## What is pinned

  Every host this app contacts on its own behalf, rather than because a user typed
  it, is nailed to HTTPS below and cannot be downgraded even though the base config
  is permissive:

    - connectivitycheck.grapheneos.network, for the 204 probe that tells "the
      monitored service is down" apart from "this phone has no working network".
      This replaced www.gstatic.com in 3.2.0. The probe works identically either
      way; what changed is that an app whose pitch is no server, no account and no
      third party is no longer telling Google's edge where the phone is once every
      forty-five seconds for a measurement nobody asked Google to be part of. The
      endpoint is user-editable, so gstatic remains reachable for anyone who wants
      it, and is simply no longer where the app goes by default.
    - api.github.com, for repository monitors and the version check. A fixed host
      this app chose rather than one the user typed, so it belongs here.
    - f-droid.org, for the version check when that is the chosen source.

  If a future release adds any other endpoint of its own, add it here the same way
  rather than letting it inherit the permissive base.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
        <trust-anchors>
            <certificates src="system" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </base-config>

    <domain-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="false">
        <domain includeSubdomains="false">connectivitycheck.grapheneos.network</domain>
        <domain includeSubdomains="false">api.github.com</domain>
        <domain includeSubdomains="false">f-droid.org</domain>
    </domain-config>
</network-security-config>
